        "step1": {
          "title": "First Look at Analog w/ Brandon Roberts",
          "duration": "5:19:21",
          "channel": "Ryan Carniato",
          "description": "The past year has really been a resurgence for Angular, between modernizing APIs with Standalone Components, embracing a new reactive model with Signals, and pushing forward in working into SSR and Hydration. But amazing work has also been happening outside of Google, with Analog, a meta-framework built on Vite for Angular. Brandon Roberts author of Analog joins us to talk about what he's been building.\n\n[0:00] Preamble\n[8:15] Welcome Brandon Roberts\n[21:30] Why Analog?\n[35:15] Angular SSR & How Angular is Changing\n[47:45] Why Not Astro?\n[51:15] Introduction to Analog\n[1:01:15] Analog Hacker News\n[1:17:15] Comment Nodes, Deferred Loading, and Bundle Size\n[1:23:15] Playing with PageSpeed Insights\n[1:34:30] The Cost of Hydration and Serialization in SSR\n[1:46:30] More Analog Features (API Routes, Meta Tags, etc.)\n[1:55:45] Analog RPC Calls? \n[2:04:30] How Nitro and Vite Work Together in Analog\n[2:16:30] Q&A: Type-Safe Loaders? Split Up Files? AngularDart?\n[2:22:30] Brandon's Parting Notes & Intermission\n[2:29:00] Bun.app & Vinxi - The Quest for a Generic Config\n[2:33:00] Quick SolidStart Benchmark Aside\n[2:40:30] People Force Ryan to Talk About HTMX\n[2:47:30] Resumability without Serialization: Introduction\n[2:56:00] RwS: Contrasting Hydration in React, Qwik, and Solid\n[3:05:00] RwS: Context, Hoisting, and Serialization\n[3:19:00] RwS: Using the Reactive Graph for Hydration & Code Splitting\n[3:38:00] RwS: How Does This Work With Islands Routing?\n[3:46:45] RwS: Is This Truly Resumability?\n[3:57:45] RwS: Is This Worth It? Is This the Future of Solid?\n[4:06:45] RwS: Conclusion\n[4:18:30] This Week in JavaScript: Solid News and Bun 0.7\n[4:24:45] TWiJ: SolidJS Fellowships & JSNation Talk\n[4:30:30] TWiJ: TanStack Router Now Has Suspense & Streaming\n[4:33:00] TWiJ: Remix & RSC's\n[4:36:45] TWiJ: Dylan Piercey Has Strong Casing Opinions\n[4:39:30] TWiJ: Lydia Hallie's React Performance Article\n[4:50:45] TWiJ: React Performance in the Benchmarks\n[4:56:00] TWiJ: Dan Abramov is Leaving Meta.\n\nThank you @zombiefacesupreme for the timestamps.",
